<svelte:head><title>Research methods — Voynich@home</title><meta name="description" content="Learn how Voynich@home defines searches, tests known messages and controls, checks submitted results, and reviews scientific claims."/></svelte:head>
<div class="page narrow prose"><p class="eyebrow">Research methods</p><h1>How a search result becomes evidence</h1><p class="lede">A campaign defines the possibilities to search, the data and computation budget, the comparisons, and the decision rule. Its complete record lets another researcher repeat the work.</p>
<h2>First, recover known messages</h2><p>Latin and Italian are a practical starting scope. Their inclusion does not imply that either is the manuscript’s language. Our search uses compact character-sequence models trained on separate source works. The decoder’s output is never rewritten into fluent prose.</p><p>The program compares ordinary substitution, homophonic substitution, and documented encodings that expand letters into groups. It tests 1,000, 5,000, and 20,000 normalized characters, and compares 1, 8, and 64 independent starts where the method supports restarts.</p><p>The completed development panel selected frozen settings. A fresh-case evaluation is now testing 100 new message-and-key cases per reported condition, with the same search budgets applied to shuffled and generated controls. No evaluation report is available yet, so the development observations remain limited.</p>
<h2>Preserve the construction</h2><p>Naibbe is a documented encoding with several Voynich-like properties. Its author does not claim that it solves the manuscript. Our implementation preserves the published tables and notices, and rejects ambiguous output. Our key-randomized extension is labeled separately: after parsing the known symbol-group structure, it reduces to substitution. Recovering that extension is not evidence of an unrestricted verbose-cipher solution.</p><p><a href="https://www.michaelgreshko.com/naibbe-cipher">Michael Greshko’s Naibbe explanation</a> · <a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/tree/33debe761229e99816cd6d0f1efc1bc65c775b4d/third_party/naibbe">Imported implementation and terms</a></p>
<h2>Then, define a manuscript search</h2><p>A manuscript campaign needs a useful operating range established by recovery tests. It must declare its exact transcription, included passages, grouping and space rules, language resources, algorithm, seeds, budget, comparisons, and stopping conditions.</p><p>Pages, lines, paragraphs, uncertainty, and excluded material remain visible. Major manuscript text groups are studied separately before combining them. A candidate includes its actual mapping and unchanged decoder output, including unparsed symbols and exceptions.</p>
<h2 id="validation">Check the execution and the candidate</h2><p>The server assigns the same task to more than one participant and independently reruns every task before awarding credit. This checks that the returned output and score match the declared computation. A disagreement receives no credit, while an interrupted task remains an operational failure with no scientific meaning.</p><p>Browser identifiers cannot establish that results came from separate people or machines. Matching execution demonstrates reproducibility under the recorded conditions; assessing a historical reading requires additional evidence and specialist review.</p><p>Credit uses a fixed work estimate: the ceiling of search evaluations × normalized ciphertext symbols ÷ 1,000. Reported runtime and the scientific interpretation do not affect it. Team totals include checked credit from current members with public profiles.</p>
<h2 id="corrections">Correct the scientific foundation</h2><p>The former “Rule C” did not justify its claimed 1% error guarantee. We removed that claim, corrected replicate-count and metric inconsistencies, and stopped interpreting an empty adaptive search as exclusion of an entire continuous parameter domain.</p><p>Earlier work used the whole manuscript during development. Current manuscript results are exploratory. For formal testing of a fully specified generator, the conditional test calibrates fresh simulated target texts with the same declared procedure; its assumptions and finite simulation limits are recorded.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/blob/33debe761229e99816cd6d0f1efc1bc65c775b4d/docs/research/acceptance-rule-review.md">Acceptance-rule review</a> · <a href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/blob/33debe761229e99816cd6d0f1efc1bc65c775b4d/docs/SCIENTIFIC-CORRECTIONS.md">Corrections and interpretation</a></p>
<h2>Review and propose</h2><p>The human project owner remains the release authority. Current development checks are maintained within the project and are described as project-controlled. Outside researchers can add independent reproduction and subject expertise.</p><p>An experiment proposal must provide:</p><ul><li>A concrete research question.</li><li>Data sources, rights, and prior exposure.</li><li>A reproducible method and fixed computation budget.</li><li>Positive, negative, and generated comparisons.</li><li>A decision rule and stopping condition.</li><li>A plan to publish unsuccessful and successful results.</li></ul><a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/javidmardanov/VoynichAtHome/issues/new?template=experiment-proposal.yml">Propose an experiment ↗</a>
</div>
